  "text": "Cell walls from a filamentous fungus were separated using chemical methods that involved an oxidizing agent or a mixture of a strong acid and a peroxide, followed by a dilute mineral acid wash. The resulting walls were examined with high‑resolution imaging and diffraction techniques, which showed that the walls of the root‑like extensions were especially clear for study. Thin fibrils made of a common structural sugar polymer were found at the very tips of these extensions and tended to line up along the length of the filament. In older sections, the walls displayed a crossed‑fibril pattern beneath a thin outer layer of short, randomly arranged fibrils, a pattern similar to that seen in certain photosynthetic organisms. Early developmental stages of branching filaments showed fibril orientations that could be explained by passive re‑alignment of existing fibers. The main filament walls typically had two layers: an outer layer with randomly placed fibrils and an inner layer where fibrils were well aligned parallel to the filament’s axis, resembling the secondary wall of a related fungal structure."
